Reason:  
• It derives the minimum / maximum track lengths from the Track table alone (so every track is considered), then joins a separate revenue sub‑query that looks only at InvoiceLine; therefore min/max are not mistakenly limited to tracks that happened to be sold.  
• The CASE logic and thresholds exactly match the “short < 3 min, medium 3–5 min, long > 5 min” definition in both sub‑queries, avoiding any category mismatch.  
• Duplicate rows from the sales side cannot distort the min/max figures because those statistics are calculated before any join.  
• The final SELECT returns the requested columns (category, min, max, total revenue) already rounded and ordered as short, medium, long.  
The other scripts (1result.sql and 2result.sql) compute min/max after joining with InvoiceLine, so they ignore tracks that have never been sold and can return higher minimum values, which does not satisfy “calculate the duration of each track.”